diff --git a/docs/decisions/622-viewcube-roll-snap.md b/docs/decisions/622-viewcube-roll-snap.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..618d0d96
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/622-viewcube-roll-snap.md
@@ -0,0 +1,48 @@
+# One PR, one file - see README.md. Collected into RECOMMENDED.md at the cut.
+
+## #622 - Pole clicks: snap the roll to the nearest quarter turn, not to +X
+
+**Decided.** `Camera::toward` keeps its law - same target, same distance,
+eye along `d` - with one extra clause: when the normalized direction is
+exactly a Z pole (`n[2].abs() == 1.0`), the yaw comes not from the
+vanishing `atan2(n[1], n[0])` (which always reads 0) but from the
+pre-click camera's own yaw snapped to the nearest quarter turn,
+`(yaw / FRAC_PI_2).round() * FRAC_PI_2`. `basis_right`'s zero-guard,
+which pinned screen-right to world +X at the poles, evaluates that yaw
+instead: `(-yaw.sin(), yaw.cos(), 0.0)`.
+
+**Why.** A view-cube TOP or BOT click from any yawed approach threw the
+roll away: every click landed screen-right on +X, spinning the model up
+to half a turn on screen, and the user's framing was gone (issue #622's
+repro and claim comment). The old `+X` pin existed because at an exact
+pole `cross(forward, +Z)` vanishes and `normalize` answers zero - some
+convention had to fill the hole. The near-pole limit says what that
+convention should be: for every `|pitch| < pi/2` the guard's own formula
+is *exactly* (to rounding) `(-sin y, cos y, 0)`, so evaluating it at the
+pole extends the same value to the one point where the cross product underflows
+(`cos(pi/2)^2 ~ 3.7e-33` trips `normalize`'s `EPSILON` floor). Snapping
+the yaw a quarter turn keeps the surviving half of the old pole law -
+screen up and right ride world axes, views sharing a quarter turn roll
+alike - while bounding a near-pole click's on-screen rotation to 45°,
+the snap's own bound, with views already on a quarter turn not moving at
+all. A second click at the same pole is exact idempotence: the yaw is
+already snapped.
+
+**Considered and dropped.**
+- Keep `+X` and roll the view down into it: state or animation inside a
+  one-line orientation law; the Prime Directive does not pay for tweens.
+- Carry the pre-click yaw to the pole un-snapped: fully continuous roll,
+  but screen up lands on an arbitrary compass bearing and the view keeps
+  the tilt the cube law promises to remove.
+- Snap whenever `abs(n[2])` merely approaches 1: `hit_dir` classifies
+  only exact face centers as poles (edge and corner picks carry x/y
+  components, their pitches well inside `asin(1/sqrt(2))`) and orbit
+  clamps pitch at 1.5 - the exact pole is the only reachable near-
+  singularity, and a near-pole branch would manufacture a discontinuity
+  where the math has none.
+
+**Cost to change later.** Two lines: one `if` in `toward`, one
+expression in the `basis_right` guard. `view_cube_tests` pins the snap
+values, the quarter-turn pole bases, the 45° bound and the idempotence;
+basis consumers (mvp, picking, pan, the cube painter) ride any
+orthonormal frame and need no touching.

From 09f5d385a3fce6610f676cd923c5018e16f625f6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tbrasselle <tbrasselle@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 21:07:45 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] fix(#622): pole clicks snap the roll to the nearest
 quarter turn

At an exact Z pole toward() read atan2(0,0)=0 and basis_right pinned
screen-right to literal +X, so clicking TOP/BOT spun a yawed view up to
half a turn on screen. Now the pole takes the pre-click yaw snapped to
the nearest FRAC_PI_2, and basis_right's zero-guard evaluates
(-sin y, cos y, 0) at that yaw - the limit every near-pole view already
rides, so the guard is continuous and a second pole click is a no-op.
docs/decisions/622-viewcube-roll-snap.md.
---
 crates/ui/src/viewport.rs | 31 +++++++++++++++++++++++--------
 1 file changed, 23 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/crates/ui/src/viewport.rs b/crates/ui/src/viewport.rs
index 7c49e454..0d707582 100644
--- a/crates/ui/src/viewport.rs
+++ b/crates/ui/src/viewport.rs
@@ -1216,11 +1216,14 @@ impl Camera {
     /// standard orientation turns yaw/pitch. Non-finite `d` is a no-op
     /// that answers `self` - the cube's classification is finite by
     /// construction, so this is the Prime Directive's belt, not a path.
-    /// The straight-up/straight-down cases need no special yaw: `d.z`
-    /// lands pitch on `±pi/2`, and `basis_right`'s zero-guard (normalize
-    /// reads a squared length below EPSILON first) pins screen-right to
-    /// world +X there - so a pole view is roll-stable whatever `atan2`
-    /// returns for a vanishing `d.x`, `d.y`.
+    /// The straight-up/straight-down cases need a special yaw: `d.x`,
+    /// `d.y` vanish there, so `atan2` answers 0 and every pole click
+    /// would land on one canonical roll, spinning a yawed approach up to
+    /// half a turn on screen (#622). Instead the pole takes the *pre-
+    /// click* yaw snapped to the nearest quarter turn - the roll the view
+    /// had, kept; `basis_right`'s zero-guard then evaluates that yaw, so
+    /// a pole view rolls like its yaw and a second pole click changes
+    /// nothing (`docs/decisions/622-viewcube-roll-snap.md`).
     #[must_use]
     pub fn toward(&self, d: [f64; 3]) -> Camera {
         if d.iter().any(|v| !v.is_finite()) {
@@ -1234,9 +1237,18 @@ impl Camera {
             return *self;
         }
         n = [n[0] / l, n[1] / l, n[2] / l];
+        // At an exact Z pole the atan2 vanishes; take the roll from the
+        // yaw this camera already had, snapped to the nearest quarter
+        // turn, so the click levels the view without turning it (#622).
+        let quarter = std::f64::consts::FRAC_PI_2;
+        let yaw = if n[2].abs() == 1.0 {
+            (self.yaw / quarter).round() * quarter
+        } else {
+            n[1].atan2(n[0])
+        };
         Camera {
             target: self.target,
-            yaw: n[1].atan2(n[0]),
+            yaw,
             pitch: n[2].clamp(-1.0, 1.0).asin(),
             dist: self.dist,
             projection: self.projection,
@@ -1288,13 +1300,16 @@ impl Camera {
         normalize([self.target[0] - e[0], self.target[1] - e[1], self.target[2] - e[2]])
     }
 
-    /// Right = normalize(cross(forward, +Z)); stable except straight up/down.
+    /// Right = normalize(cross(forward, +Z)); at an exact Z pole the cross
+    /// vanishes, and the guard evaluates the same expression at the camera's
+    /// (quarter-snapped, see `toward`) yaw instead - the limit every
+    /// near-pole view already rides (#622).
     #[must_use]
     fn basis_right(&self) -> (f64, f64, f64) {
         let (fx, fy, _) = self.fwd();
         let (x, y, _) = normalize([fy, -fx, 0.0]);
         if x == 0.0 && y == 0.0 {
-            return (1.0, 0.0, 0.0); // looking straight along Z
+            return (-self.yaw.sin(), self.yaw.cos(), 0.0); // looking straight along Z
         }
         (x, y, 0.0)
     }
